{"name":"Tourmaline Turquoise","hex":"#4f9e96","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tourmaline","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A complex black or dark-coloured borosilicate mineral, compounded with various chemical elements and considered a semi-precious stone.","A transparent gemstone cut from it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tourmaline"},{"term":"turquoise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone.","A pale greenish-blue colour, like that of the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of turquoise (the gemstone).","Having a pale greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turquoise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tourmaline","blocks":[{"text":"Tourmaline is a crystalline silicate mineral group in which boron is compounded with elements such as aluminium, iron, magnesium, sodium, lithium, or potassium. This gemstone comes in a wide variety of colors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Tourmaline-121240.jpg/330px-Tourmaline-121240.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tourmaline","wikidata":"Q179911"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise","wikidata":"Q165254"},{"title":"Turquoise (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is a cyan color, based on the mineral of the same name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise_(c